Over the last months, I've been reflecting on my career, how I position what it is I do and how I use this site to communicate it. ("Person who is good at translating between technical and non-technical stakeholders" is a useful skill, but not what I do, nor a marketable term). I decided to scratch that itch by looking through some of my old online presences, the websites and the photo archives, expecting mostly nostalgia. But I found new clarity to the same question I work on now: how should information be arranged so someone can find it, understand it and connect it to something else?

The record I can recover runs from 2002 to 2026. The tools changed from static HTML and Flash to content models, semantic embeddings and server-rendered SVG. Reading it back gave this blog a structure it has always needed.

## Brace yourself for some meditative navel gazing

My first website was a GeoCities page I made in high school around 1997. I may never find it again. My memory is that I first edited HTML in HotDog, a Windows editor [released in 1995](https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/software/hotdog-1-0-in-1995).

What I remember is not the page but the reaction. A friend (Melissa, who I don't think I've spoken to since graduation) and I found it genuinely mindbending that we could make a thing and have it be *there*, addressable by anyone, without asking permission or paying a gatekeeper.

The first substantial site I can recover is Environmental Media. The Wayback Machine kept a [2002 snapshot of the site](https://web.archive.org/web/20020120145620/https://envmedia.com/), which I built in 2001 with search, Flash video and an online shop. The implementation is very much of its time; the interest in helping people navigate different kinds of information is less so.

An [October 2002 snapshot of Stuffed in a Box](https://web.archive.org/web/20021012212136/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/) preserves a mostly "Coming Soon" site for my tiny web-design service. By April 2003, an [online résumé](https://web.archive.org/web/20030425093346/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/portfolio/resume/index.htm) listed Stuffed in a Box Design as a self-owned business and used proper em dashes — way before AI killed being a cool kid who knew how to.

The blog arrived on [5 September 2003](https://web.archive.org/web/20050206010037/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/archives/000004.html). Its first post, "I've done and will do," promised observations about newspapers, infographics, technology, politics and history. A post the following September admitted that it had been nearly a year since the last one, but the intended subjects are recognizable.

Flickr supplies more breadcrumbs, two of them a month apart. In November 2006 I posted [a newspaper page I had laid out](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/301936689/): every school in five South Carolina districts, rated across four years of state report cards.

South Carolina published words rather than numbers: excellent, good, average, below average, unsatisfactory, for every school, four years running. Words do not stack up. You cannot skim two hundred of them and come away with a shape. Stars you can count, and counting is what a parent looking for their own school actually does.

A month later I uploaded [a close-up of a pinched letterform](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/337710045/): I was already stopping to admire ink traps in 2006. Something I found myself dwelling on again twelve years later, [when considering fonts for digital data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html).

The next many years would be consumed by [TheDigitel.com](https://www.TheDigitel.com) an excellent if poorly timed idea (2008 was bad for the entire economy, including advertiser budgets) for hyperlocal news that I co-founded. I won't digress into that, it's a full-on post.

## Reading the archive instead of scrolling it

I finally parted ways with TheDigitel in 2011, taking a job offer in Germany, and resurfaced writing on this site in 2013. It now contains roughly a hundred entries written on and off across different jobs, tools and versions of the site. That's a mineable data pool which I can compare topics and find passages that resemble one another. It is also enough for a conventional reverse-chronological blog to hide most of what is there.

This 2026 redesign began with typography, graph paper and a more technical datasheet vocabulary. Posts gained document references. The [archive gained a timeline](/archive/), while [topic pages](/directory/#subjects) and small activity charts made it possible to browse by subject rather than only by publication date.

