## Similarity, meaningfully

This site has a [semantic search index](/posts/20260302-semantic-search-browser-embeddings/) which I've used to connect repeated language and adjacent subject matter. It does not show that one idea caused another, that an employer shaped a topic, or even that I consciously remembered an older post while writing a newer one. It also only knows what I have published here: drafts, navigation and everything off-site never enter it, so its totals sit below the topic counts and the decade before 2013 barely registers. The map is candid about its own gaps, and that turns out to be the useful part.

Compounding this, much of the meaning I'd like to draw from still lives elsewhere:

- more than a thousand photographs in [my Flickr archive](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/), which should not remain their only home;
- writing published through employers, projects and publications that now lives on sites I do not control;
- small tools and experiments spread across [my GitHub repositories](https://github.com/khawkins98);
- older versions of this site preserved unevenly in Git history and the Wayback Machine.

The role I want for this site is an index across them: photographs, projects and recovered articles, each carrying its date, its original context and a link back to where it first appeared.

It also means accepting gaps. I do not want to manufacture a continuous personal history from incomplete evidence. "I cannot find this" is useful archive metadata. So is "this only survives as a screenshot" or "the original link is gone."

Photography is the part I most want to bring back as an active practice, not only a rescue operation. The current archive is heavily weighted toward words and software.

The immediate work is unglamorous: recover what I can with dates and sources intact, and give photographs and projects a shape of their own instead of forcing them into blog-post templates.

The site has been rebuilt several times: Drupal, [Gulp](/posts/20170725-site-update.html), [Panini](/posts/20180210-zurb-panini-2-migration.html), [Eleventy](/posts/20200713-moving-from-panini-to-eleventy/), then a [much simpler Eleventy stack](/posts/20250831-site-overhaul-eleventy-v3/), and now a semantic overhaul. Each migration focused on the machinery. This rebuild started with a question about myself instead: how to say what I do without reaching for "person who translates between technical and non-technical people," which is a real skill and a hopeless title.

I have settled, for now, on Digital Platforms & Audience Engagement Lead, but the honest version is the through line the record keeps showing me: not a platform, not a publishing habit, not really a job title, but my repeated efforts to make information legible, navigable and connected, for the people reading it and increasingly for the machines reading on their behalf.

The fresh coat of paint is that same conviction turned on my own record: the datasheet vocabulary, the timeline and the Story Lab are not decoration but an attempt to make this collection legible rather than merely recent. The site should do the work, not just describe it.

Twenty years ago I turned two hundred words the state would not add up into a column of stars a parent could count at a glance. This is the same move, pointed at myself: taking a career that only ever arrived one post at a time and arranging it so the shape is finally countable. That is why the archive is worth keeping: enough context to see the shape, and enough structure to keep following it.

